Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (Bodies and content, Exclusion scope, Schema version; supports AC45, AC49, AC50, AC51, AC52)
Research: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/research.md (Q5: tool_call_id)
Why this approach: encoding/json over map[string]any sorts keys for free; UseNumber keeps numbers as sent; exclusions run on the decoded tree at one level (block object, or each entry of the system/tools arrays) and never on tool input, result content or raw JSON. Tool blocks take their {id, name, input_hash} / {tool_call_id, is_error, content_hash} from the first matching tool event after the message, and the message and reference objects are stored as Content so every hash resolves.
Alternatives rejected: hashing the raw bytes (key order and whitespace would defeat dedup); putting index, source or stop_reason in the message hash (a resent copy would never match); tolerating a tool block with no tool event (hides parser bugs).
Trade-off / known limit: invalid JSON in any RawMessage, a Kind with no payload, or a tool block with no matching event is an error for the whole exchange's parse; the payload shape is recorded in plan.md. Re-encoding normalises string escapes (e.g. é becomes the UTF-8 character), which is intended.
